---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";

import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE } from "../lib/site";

import empty from "../assets/manual/empty.png";
import main from "../assets/manual/main.png";
import sentence from "../assets/manual/sentence.png";
import parentCheck from "../assets/manual/parent-check.png";
import editMode from "../assets/manual/edit-mode.png";
import moreMenu from "../assets/manual/more-menu.png";
import wordEditor from "../assets/manual/word-editor.png";
import templates from "../assets/manual/templates.png";
import importConfirm from "../assets/manual/import-confirm.png";
import voicePrompt from "../assets/manual/voice-prompt.png";
import voiceList from "../assets/manual/voice-list.png";
import tamilFolder from "../assets/manual/tamil-folder.png";
import languageList from "../assets/manual/language-list.png";
import keyboard from "../assets/manual/keyboard.png";
import builder from "../assets/manual/builder.png";

const shot = {
  widths: [640, 1100],
  sizes: "(max-width: 58rem) 100vw, 58rem",
  class: "shot",
};
---

<Base
  title="User manual"
  description="How to use GibTalk: building sentences, adding your own words and pictures, languages and voices, templates, backups, and printing boards."
>
  <h1>User manual</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Everything GibTalk does, step by step. The screenshots are from an Android
    tablet; phones, iPhones and iPads work the same way.
  </p>

  <nav class="toc card" aria-label="Contents">
    <ol>
      <li>
        <a href="#talking">Talking with GibTalk</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#edit-mode">Edit mode</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#words">Adding and changing words</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#languages">Languages and voices</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#keyboard">Typing with the keyboard</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#templates">Templates</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#backup">Backup, restore and new devices</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#builder">Building and printing on a computer</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#tips">Tips for starting out</a>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="talking">1. Talking with GibTalk</h2>
  <h3>The first time</h3>
  <p>
    A new install starts with no words. To get going, either add your own words
    in <a href="#edit-mode">edit mode</a>, add a ready-made
    <a href="#templates">template</a>, or restore a<a href="#backup">backup</a>{" "}
    from another device.
  </p>
  <Image
    src={empty}
    alt="GibTalk on first launch: an empty board with a message explaining how to add words, restore a backup or add a template"
    {...shot}
  />

  <h3>The Home board</h3>
  <p>
    Once it has words, GibTalk opens on the <strong>Home</strong> board, a grid
    of picture tiles. This one was set up for a child at home and at school:
  </p>
  <Image
    src={main}
    alt="The Home board: yellow folder tiles such as Greetings, Food and Toys, green word tiles such as Yes, No, Stop and Help, and buttons around the edge"
    {...shot}
  />
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Green tiles are words.</strong> Tap one and GibTalk says it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Yellow tiles are folders.</strong> Tap one to open it and see the
      words inside, for example <em>Food</em>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Back</strong> goes up one folder, <strong>Home</strong> goes
      straight back to the Home board. The line under the buttons (Home / Food)
      shows where you are; tap a name in it to jump there.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Making a sentence</h3>
  <p>
    Each tile you tap is added to the bar at the top, so a child can build a
    sentence one picture at a time.
  </p>
  <Image
    src={sentence}
    alt="Inside the Food folder, the top bar holds three tapped words: Makan, Nasi Goreng, Lagi"
    {...shot}
  />
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Play</strong> says the whole sentence.
    </li>
    <li>Tap a word in the bar to hear just that word.</li>
    <li>
      The <strong>⌫</strong> button removes the last word. Press and hold it to
      remove them all.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Clear All</strong> also empties the bar.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="edit-mode">2. Edit mode</h2>
  <p>
    Changes happen in edit mode, behind a simple check so children don't change
    words by accident. Tap <strong>Edit</strong>, type the four numbers shown,
    and tap <strong>Ok</strong>. (On a tablet, close the on-screen keyboard
    first if it covers the Ok button.)
  </p>
  <Image
    src={parentCheck}
    alt="A box over the board says 'Please input 3075 to continue', with a text field and an Ok button"
    {...shot}
  />
  <p>In edit mode, each tile gets three small buttons:</p>
  <Image
    src={editMode}
    alt="The Home board in edit mode: each tile has a pencil and a copy button at the top left and an X at the top right; Add, More and Done buttons are on the right"
    {...shot}
  />
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Pencil:</strong> change the word (see below).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Copy:</strong> puts the word in the <em>Clipboard</em> bar at the
      top. Open another folder and tap <strong>Paste</strong> to add a copy
      there. Copy several words and paste them together.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>X:</strong> deletes the word, or the folder and everything in it.{" "}
      <strong>There is no undo</strong>, so make a<a href="#backup">backup</a>{" "}
      before big changes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Press and hold</strong> a tile, then drag it, to move it to a new
      place.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    On the right: <strong>Add</strong> makes a new word in the folder you're in,{" "}
    <strong>More</strong> has Templates, Backup and Restore, and
    <strong>Done</strong> leaves edit mode.
  </p>
  <Image
    src={moreMenu}
    alt="The More menu open beside the buttons, listing Templates, Backup and Restore"
    {...shot}
  />

  <h2 id="words">3. Adding and changing words</h2>
  <p>
    Tap <strong>Add</strong> for a new word, or the pencil on a tile to change
    one. Both open the same screen:
  </p>
  <Image
    src={wordEditor}
    alt="The Edit Word screen: Label field, Language list, a Category switch, a Save button, and on the right the picture with three buttons above it"
    {...shot}
  />
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Label:</strong> the word as it should be spoken and shown.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Language:</strong> which voice reads it (see
      <a href="#languages">Languages and voices</a>).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Category?</strong> Switch on to make this tile a folder that can
      hold other words.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Picture:</strong> the three buttons pick a photo from the device,
      take a new photo with the camera, or search the free symbol library.
      Photos of the child's own cup, food or family often work better than
      symbols.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Tap <strong>Save</strong> when you're done.
  </p>

  <h2 id="languages">4. Languages and voices</h2>
  <p>
    GibTalk doesn't have its own voices. It uses the text-to-speech voices on
    your phone or tablet, so it can speak any language your device has a voice
    for, and each word can use a different language. A board can mix Malay,
    English, Tamil and Chinese, and a sentence switches voice from word to word.
  </p>

  <h3>Write words in the language's own script</h3>
  <p>
    The voice reads the letters it's given. A Tamil voice reads Tamil script, so
    write <strong>அம்மா</strong>, not <em>Amma</em>; the same goes for Chinese
    characters. A word typed in English letters but set to Tamil won't sound
    right. If you can't type the script, ask someone who can, or start from a{" "}
    <a href="#templates">template</a> that already has it.
  </p>
  <Image
    src={tamilFolder}
    alt="A folder of Tamil words in Tamil script, with three of them in the sentence bar"
    {...shot}
  />

  <h3>Choosing a word's language</h3>
  <p>
    In the word screen, tap <strong>Language</strong>. The list shows the
    languages your device has a voice for. If yours isn't there, tap
    <strong>Add a language…</strong> to install a voice.
  </p>
  <Image
    src={languageList}
    alt="The language list: English, Indonesia, Melayu, Tamil (ticked), Chinese, and Add a language…"
    {...shot}
  />

  <h3>Installing a voice</h3>
  <p>
    When words use a language with no voice on the device, GibTalk says so and
    offers <strong>Install voice</strong>. Until a voice is installed, those
    words are read by another voice. The <strong>Edit</strong> button also shows
    a dot, and edit mode shows a reminder, until it's fixed.
  </p>
  <Image
    src={voicePrompt}
    alt="A message: 'Some words use Tamil, but this device has no voice for it yet', with Not now and Install voice buttons"
    {...shot}
  />
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Android:</strong> <em>Install voice</em> opens the voice list of
      your text-to-speech engine (usually Speech Services by Google). Find the
      language, for example <em>Tamil (India)</em>, and download it (about 10
      MB). Then come back to GibTalk. If the list doesn't open, go to Settings,
      search for "Text-to-speech output", open the settings of the preferred
      engine and install the voice data there.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>iPhone and iPad:</strong> Settings → Accessibility → Spoken
      Content → Voices, choose the language and download a voice. Apple doesn't
      offer a voice for every language; if yours isn't listed, the device can't
      speak it yet.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Image
    src={voiceList}
    alt="Android's Add a language screen for Speech Services by Google, listing languages with download sizes, including Tamil (India)"
    {...shot}
  />

  <h3>Example: trying Tamil</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>
      In edit mode, tap <strong>More → Templates</strong> and choose
      <strong>வாழ்த்துகள்</strong> (Basic Greetings in Tamil).
    </li>
    <li>
      If GibTalk asks to install a voice, tap <strong>Install voice</strong>,
      download Tamil, and come back.
    </li>
    <li>
      Tap <strong>Done</strong>, open the new folder at the end of Home, and tap
      a word.
    </li>
    <li>
      To add your own Tamil words, tap <strong>Add</strong>, type the word in
      Tamil script, and set <strong>Language</strong> to தமிழ் (Tamil).
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="keyboard">5. Typing with the keyboard</h2>
  <p>
    For anything that isn't on the board, tap <strong>Keyboard</strong>, type,
    and tap <strong>Play</strong>. The button beside the text picks the language
    (here <em>ms</em> for Malay). <strong>Words</strong> goes back to the
    pictures.
  </p>
  <Image
    src={keyboard}
    alt="The keyboard screen: a text field with 'selamat pagi cikgu', a language button showing ms, and Play and Clear All buttons"
    {...shot}
  />

  <h2 id="templates">6. Templates</h2>
  <p>
    Templates are ready-made sets of words, such as greetings, mealtime, the
    playground, school or feelings, in English, Malay and Tamil. In edit mode,
    tap
    <strong>More → Templates</strong> and pick one. On a computer, you can see
    and print any of them in the <a href="/builder/">template builder</a>
    (Start from a template…).
  </p>
  <Image
    src={templates}
    alt="The Import a Template screen: Import from file at the top, then a list of templates such as Greetings, Ucapan and the Tamil greetings"
    {...shot}
  />
  <p>
    A template is <strong>added</strong> to your words, as a new folder at the
    end of Home; nothing you already have is changed.
  </p>
  <Image
    src={importConfirm}
    alt="A confirmation: 'Import template into current board? Words in this template will be combined with your current board', with Cancel and Ok"
    {...shot}
  />
  <p>
    <strong>Import from file</strong> at the top of the same screen adds the
    words from a file: a backup, a set made in the
    <a href="/builder/">template builder</a>, or a file someone sent you.
  </p>

  <h2 id="backup">7. Backup, restore and new devices</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Backup</strong> (More → Backup) makes one file with all your words
      and pictures and opens the share menu. Save it to your files, Google Drive
      or iCloud, or send it to yourself.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Restore</strong> (More → Restore) opens a backup file and
      <strong>replaces every word</strong> on the device with the ones in the
      file.
    </li>
    <li>
      To <strong>add</strong> words from a file without losing what's there, use{" "}
      <strong>Templates → Import from file</strong> instead.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Moving to a new phone or tablet:</strong> make a backup on the old
    device, send the file to the new one, install GibTalk there, and restore it.
    Backups work between Android and iPhone/iPad.
  </p>
  <p>
    GibTalk keeps everything on the device and has no account, so a backup is
    the only copy outside the device. Make one now and then, and before big
    changes.
  </p>

  <h2 id="builder">8. Building and printing on a computer</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/builder/">template builder</a> works in a browser on a laptop
    or computer, with a bigger screen and a keyboard. It's handy for teachers
    and therapists preparing words for several children.
  </p>
  <Image
    src={builder}
    alt="The template builder: a name field, buttons to download, open a file or start from a template, the print row with rows and columns, and the words grid"
    widths={[640, 925]}
    sizes="(max-width: 58rem) 100vw, 58rem"
    class="shot"
  />
  <ul>
    <li>
      Add words and folders, and pick symbols or upload photos, much like in the
      app. You can also start from a template or open a backup.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Download file</strong>, send it to the device, and use
      <strong>Templates → Import from file</strong> (adds the words) or
      <strong>Restore</strong> (replaces all words).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Printing:</strong> choose the rows and columns, for example 6 × 9
      for a core board. <strong>Print this folder</strong> prints the folder on
      screen as one board; <strong>Print all as a book</strong> puts each folder
      on its own page, with page numbers to follow. Pages are A4 landscape, and
      the print window can save a PDF. Printed boards are useful in class,
      outdoors, or when the tablet is charging.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="tips">9. Tips for starting out</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Start small: a few words the child wants every day (eat, drink, toilet,
      help, more, stop), then add more as they're used.
    </li>
    <li>
      Put the most-used words on Home, and group the rest into folders by place
      or activity.
    </li>
    <li>
      Use the words yourself: tap them as you talk, so the child sees how the
      board works.
    </li>
    <li>Keep the same words in the same place, so they're easy to find.</li>
    <li>Make a backup once the board is set up.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Questions or a problem? See <a href="/support/">Support</a> or email
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.supportEmail}`}>{SITE.supportEmail}</a>.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  .lead {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }
  .toc ol {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.4rem;
  }
  :global(.shot) {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    margin: 1rem 0;
    border: 3px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 16px;
  }
  h2 {
    scroll-margin-top: 1rem;
  }
</style>
